  <!-- EN: Battle Simulation Modifiers -->
  <Codex_BattleSimulation.label>TODO</Codex_BattleSimulation.label>
  <!-- EN: Both auto-defend battles and offensive raids involving Empire settlements are resolved by simulation. Two settings control how that simulation behaves.\n\nDefender Advantage is a flat multiplier on the defender's effective combat power. The default reflects the natural edge that comes from prepared positions; raising it makes garrisons more durable, lowering it makes attackers more deadly.\n\nEfficiency Damping compresses extreme efficiency ratios toward 1.0, so neither side runs away with the fight on a single lucky composition. The formula is straightforward: final = 1 + (raw − 1) × damping. A raw efficiency of 2.0 with the default damping of 0.5 becomes 1.5, and a raw of 0.3 with the same damping becomes 0.65.\n\nSet damping high to preserve the raw outcomes (sharp wins and sharp losses); set it low to flatten the curve (no battle is ever a runaway). Defender Advantage and Damping interact, so tune both together rather than in isolation.\n<i><color=#66E0E4FF>Empire Refactored</color></i> -->
  <Codex_BattleSimulation.description>TODO</Codex_BattleSimulation.description>
  
  <!-- EN: Battle Simulation -->
  <Codex_BattleSimulation_AutoBattles.label>TODO</Codex_BattleSimulation_AutoBattles.label>
  <!-- EN: Both auto-defend battles and offensive raids involving Empire settlements are resolved by simulation.\n            \nAn auto-resolve battle begins by assigning both the attacker and the defender an HP value, derived from their base Military Level and any modifiers (such as Military Efficiency or Defender Advantage).\n            \nThe simulation then consists of a number of rounds. Each round, the attacker and defender roll a d20; the side with the lowest value loses one HP, with the defender winning ties. This repeats until one side reaches 0 HP.\n            \nBut there's a catch: each d20 roll is multiplied by that force's Military Efficiency. For a force with an efficiency of 1.2, a d20 roll of 10 becomes 12; meanwhile, for a force with an efficiency of 0.8, a d20 roll of 10 becomes 8.\n            \nIn this way, military efficiency can have a substantial impact on auto-resolve battles. Don't neglect it! And mind that technologically advanced factions have higher military efficiency - Spacer-tech factions have an efficiency of 1.2, Ultra-tech has 1.3, and Archotech has 1.5!\n            \nThe effect of Military Efficiency can be dampened through the Efficiency Damping mod setting. More on that in the Battle Simulation Modifiers entry.\n<i><color=#66E0E4FF>Empire Refactored</color></i> -->
  <Codex_BattleSimulation_AutoBattles.description>TODO</Codex_BattleSimulation_AutoBattles.description>
  
  <!-- EN: Difficulty Settings -->
  <Codex_DifficultySettings.label>TODO</Codex_DifficultySettings.label>
  <!-- EN: The Empire difficulty preset is independent of RimWorld's storyteller difficulty. It controls four economic parameters that together set how forgiving the early game is and how lucrative a mature empire becomes.\n\n - Silver per Resource: the silver value of one unit of produced resource at tax time.\n - Days between Tax Cycles: how often production is collected and converted to silver.\n - Production Tithe: a per-worker budget bonus that only counts towards tithing.\n - Worker Cost: the per-worker silver charge applied each cycle.\n\nLower presets (Peaceful, Community Builder) pay you more per resource, take a smaller cut, and charge less per worker. Higher presets (Blood and Dust, Losing is Fun) invert all four: lower silver per resource, larger cut taken, more expensive workers, and the gap between paydays is widened.\n\nChoose Custom to set each parameter independently. Useful when you want, for example, vanilla payouts but a faster tax cadence, or harsh worker costs without lowering production value.\n<i><color=#66E0E4FF>Empire Refactored</color></i> -->
  <Codex_DifficultySettings.description>TODO</Codex_DifficultySettings.description>
  
