  <!-- EN: Resource Production -->
  <Codex_ResourceProduction.label>TODO</Codex_ResourceProduction.label>
  <!-- EN: Every settlement turns assigned workers into resources through a three-stage cascade. Knowing where each stage's bonuses come from is the difference between a settlement that breaks even and one that funds the rest of your empire.\n\nStage 1: Base. Each resource starts with a per-settlement base value driven mostly by the biome and adjusted by tile mutators or landmarks. Buildings, policies, and events can add flat amounts here through additive resource-production stats.\n\nStage 2: Multiplier. Buildings, policies, governors, events, and faction-wide stat modifiers stack into a single multiplier on the base.\n\nStage 3: Workers and prosperity. The base × multiplier is then scaled linearly by assigned workers, and finally multiplied by prosperity / 100. A settlement at 50 prosperity produces half of what its workers and buildings would otherwise yield, no matter how good the rest of the chain looks.\n\nAt tax time, the produced resources are converted to silver at the configured silver-per-resource ratio, then the production tithe modifier is applied to determine what actually reaches your stockpile.\n<i><color=#66E0E4FF>Empire Refactored</color></i> -->
  <Codex_ResourceProduction.description>TODO</Codex_ResourceProduction.description>
  
  <!-- EN: Production Snapshots -->
  <Codex_ResourceProduction_Snapshot.label>TODO</Codex_ResourceProduction_Snapshot.label>
  <!-- EN: The basics of resource production are outlined in the Resource Production entry, and explain how the raw income value for each resource is calculated. However, at tax time, you may receive a different value from what the raw income, or even the gross income claims. This is due to Production Snapshotting.\n            \nEach day, a snapshot is taken of each settlement's production allocations. At tax time, you actually receive the average of these daily snapshots over the tax period.\n            \nThis means that anything that changes a resource's production - events, changed worker assignments, new buildings, etc. - will change the actual value that you receive at tax time.\n            \nYou can see the running average by mousing over a resource's raw income value in the settlement window.\n<i><color=#66E0E4FF>Empire Refactored</color></i> -->
  <Codex_ResourceProduction_Snapshot.description>TODO</Codex_ResourceProduction_Snapshot.description>
  
  <!-- EN: Resource Tithing -->
  <Codex_ResourceProduction_Tithing.label>TODO</Codex_ResourceProduction_Tithing.label>
  <!-- EN: By default, resource production is turned into silver, which is then shipped to you. But there is another way to direct a resource's production: through tithes.\n            \nTithes allow you to divert resource production towards producing actual goods. The resource's gross income is reduced, and in exchange, you can choose a list of items for the settlement to send you at tax time.\n            \nThe list of available items will depend on your tech level and unlocked research. For items that support it, you can choose the "stuff" that they're made out of, or their quality - with a respective increase on the item's price.\n            \nYou can also choose to enable Random Tithes. If enabled, you will be sent a random assortment of items, selected from the random tithes list and with a total value equal to or less than your allowed Random Tithe Budget. If the budget isn't high enough to support any single item, then it rolls over to the next tax period.\n<i><color=#66E0E4FF>Empire Refactored</color></i> -->
  <Codex_ResourceProduction_Tithing.description>TODO</Codex_ResourceProduction_Tithing.description>
  
  <!-- EN: Empire Threat Level (ETL) -->
  <Codex_ThreatLevel.label>TODO</Codex_ThreatLevel.label>
  <!-- EN: The Empire Threat Level (ETL) is a hidden multiplier (1.0x to 3.0x by default) that scales how dangerous enemy attacks are against your settlements.\n\nIt is weighted by four factors:\n - Average settlement level (35% weight)\n - Highest settlement level (15% weight)\n - Faction income (30% weight)\n - Settlement count (20% weight)\n\nEach factor follows a non-linear curve; early growth raises threat slowly, but large empires with high income hit the cap fast. Having one high-level settlement raises threat less than having many mid-level ones.\n\nDuring your first year, a handicap limits how fast threat can grow regardless of your empire's strength. After one year, the cap transitions smoothly to ETL-based scaling.\n\nThe Max Threat Multiplier setting (default 3.0x) provides an absolute ceiling to ETL.\n<i><color=#66E0E4FF>Empire Refactored</color></i> -->
